---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import PhoneLink from '../../../components/ui/PhoneLink.astro';
import WhatsAppCTA from '../../../components/ui/WhatsAppCTA';
import { siteConfig } from '../../../config/site';

const { phone, url } = siteConfig;

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'The Counting House', url: `${url}/case-studies/counting-house` },
  { name: 'Sash Windows', url: `${url}/case-studies/counting-house/sash-windows` },
];

const articleProps = {
  headline: 'The Counting House — Sash Window Restoration',
  url: `${url}/case-studies/counting-house/sash-windows`,
  datePublished: '2024-01-01',
};
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Counting House Sash Window Restoration"
  description="Sash window restoration at The Counting House: cord renewal, splice repairs, draught-proofing and original frame retention."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  articleProps={articleProps}
>
  <section class="py-16 lg:py-24 bg-paper">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <p class="text-oak uppercase tracking-wide mb-4">The Counting House — Pontefract</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl lg:text-5xl text-ink mb-6">Sash Window Restoration</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-ink/70 max-w-2xl">
        Cord replacement, splice repairs to decayed cills and rails, draught-proofing, and easing across the sash windows of a listed period building.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="py-16 bg-bone">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 space-y-10">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">The condition</h2>
        <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
          The sash windows at The Counting House had accumulated several common problems over years without specialist attention. Multiple windows had perished cords — some had dropped one sash entirely, others were operating on a single cord with the weight on one side. Several cills and bottom rails showed localised rot from water sitting in the sill zone. Paint had been applied over decades in some areas, partially sealing staff beads to frames and making opening difficult.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink/70">
          No windows required full replacement. The box frames were structurally sound, the majority of the sash members were in good condition, and the historic glazing was intact. The problems were all repairable.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">The decision process</h2>
        <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
          Each window was assessed individually. Probe testing established the extent of sound timber in the cill and rail zones. Moisture readings identified where active damp was present and where it was historic. The assessment for each window resulted in a condition note and a recommended scope.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink/70">
          On the windows with the worst cill decay, the recommendation was to splice repair the affected sections rather than replace the full cill. The remaining sound parent timber provided a good base for the repair and retaining it was the right call structurally and on heritage grounds.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">The work</h2>
        <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
          Cord replacement followed the same sequence on each window: staff beads removed carefully to avoid damage, lower sash removed, old cords cut out, pockets opened, weights inspected and serviced, new solid-braid cord threaded through pulleys and tied off to the weights, sash refitted, and staff beads refixed. On windows where the parting bead was in poor condition, it was renewed at the same time.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
          Splice repairs to decayed cill sections: the decay was cut back to sound timber, the cut made at an appropriate angle for a well-bedded scarf joint, profile-matched new timber fitted, bedded, and fixed. The joint was detailed to shed water rather than hold it. The finish was matched before the window was refitted.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink/70">
          Draught-proofing brush seals were fitted into routed slots on the meeting rail and in the jambs on the windows where the owner requested it. This was done without alteration to the external appearance of the window.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">The result</h2>
        <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
          Every sash window on the building now operates smoothly on both sashes. The frames and cills are in sound condition with the moisture cause addressed. Original frames, glazing, and hardware are all retained. The building's listed character is unchanged.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink/70">
          The repair work is not invisible — no repair to 150-year-old timber is ever truly invisible on day one. But it is sympathetic. A year or two of weathering closes the remaining visual gap further.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-6 pt-4 border-t border-ink/10">
        <a href="/case-studies/counting-house" class="text-oak font-display">← Back to Counting House</a>
        <a href="/services/sash-window-repair" class="text-oak font-display">Sash Window Repair service →</a>
        <a href="/repair-or-replace" class="text-oak font-display">Repair or Replace? →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="py-16 bg-paper">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 text-center">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl text-ink mb-4">Sash windows on a period property?</h2>
      <p class="text-ink/70 mb-8 max-w-2xl mx-auto">Send photos of the affected windows and we will advise on repair viability before arranging a site visit.</p>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
        <WhatsAppCTA client:load />
        <PhoneLink phone={phone.tel} display={phone.display} variant="secondary" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
